  "hint": "Write $16$ as a power of $2$ so both sides match.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "Write $16$ as a power of $2$ so both sides match."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "$16 = 2^4$, so $2^{x+1} = 2^4$, which means $x + 1 = 4$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$x + 1 = 4$ gives $x = 3$."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "Make the bases match: $16 = 2^4$.",
    "Now $2^{x+1} = 2^4$, so the exponents are equal: $x + 1 = 4$.",
    "Subtract $1$: $x = 3$. Check: $2^{3+1} = 2^4 = 16$."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Test whole numbers",
      "steps": [
        "Try $x = 2$: $2^3 = 8$, too small.",
        "Try $x = 3$: $2^4 = 16$ — exactly right.",
        "So $x = 3$."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Split the power first",
      "steps": [
        "$2^{x+1}$ is the same as $2^x \\times 2^1$, or $2 \\cdot 2^x$.",
        "So $2 \\cdot 2^x = 16$, which gives $2^x = 8$.",
        "$2^3 = 8$, so $x = 3$."
      ]
    }
  ],
